; Abstraction refinement clauses are permanent, and the relief valve is told so.
;
; The valve rebuilds a session from scratch once the solver is past a size and
; most of what it carries belongs to content no longer on the stack, and
; deadness is measured by clause mass. Refinement clauses used to be charged
; to the shape of the stack that provoked them, which is right for an array
; read axiom -- it is owed to that stack and really is dead once the stack is
; gone -- and wrong for these. An abstraction's clauses are definitional facts
; about the circuit, true whatever is asserted, never retracted, and paid for
; by every later solve. Charged to a stack-shaped owner they were read as dead
; the moment the stack changed shape.
;
; Thirty solves over one live multiplication, each pinning both operands to
; fresh values so the record is refined once per query and the mis-attributed
; mass accumulates across thirty different stack shapes. With the clauses
; charged to the owner that was current when they were made, the valve reads a
; session that has retracted nothing as mostly dead and re-encodes it; with
; them charged where the rest of the permanent content goes, it does not. The
; same file with the abstraction off does not re-encode either, which is the
; other half of the A/B: it is the accounting that moved, not the query.
;
; --incremental-reencode-limit=1 removes the variable floor so the ratio is
; the only thing being tested.
;
; RUN: %solver --incremental=on -s --incremental-reencode-limit=1 --bv-term-abstraction=1 --bv-term-abstraction-schemas=0 --bv-term-abstraction-compare=0 --bv-term-abstraction-rounds=5 %s 2>&1 | %OutputCheck --check-prefix=ABSTRACTED %s
; RUN: %solver --incremental=on -s --incremental-reencode-limit=1 %s 2>&1 | %OutputCheck --check-prefix=PLAIN %s
;
; ABSTRACTED-NOT: re-encoded from scratch
; ABSTRACTED: ^sat$
;
; PLAIN-NOT: re-encoded from scratch
; PLAIN: ^sat$
